## Before You Start: What You Actually Need (and What You Don't)

Three items. That is the entire kit required for beginner nail art: a base coat, a top coat, and at least two polishes in colours that work together. Everything else in this guide is a household substitute.

Here is what replaces a nail art kit for nail art without tools:

- **Toothpick** = dotting tool (small dot, detail work, dragging polish)
- **Bobby pin end** = larger dotting tool (bigger dots, flower centres)
- **Scotch tape or washi tape** = striping tape (straight lines, geometric blocks, half-moon guides)
- **Makeup sponge or corner of a kitchen sponge** = ombre sponge (gradient blending)

All 24 designs in this guide use only these four substitutes. You do not need a liner brush, a nail art pen, or any specialist equipment. For a full nail prep habit before you start, see our <a href='/topics/nail-care-guide'>nail care routine</a>  a clean, moisturised nail holds polish longer and gives you a better base to work on.

Nail prep matters more than the design itself. Shape your nails before you start (file in one direction, not back and forth), push back any cuticle overhang, and wipe the nail plate with acetone or nail polish remover to remove any oil. Oil is the main reason polish lifts at the edges within a day.

One base coat, let it dry. That is the starting point for every design in this guide. According to <a href='https://ericasata.com/blogs/news/easy-nail-art-designs-for-beginners'>beginner nail art guidance</a> from minimalist nail specialists, base coat adhesion is the single factor most beginners skip and most regret.

